    <h1 id="about">MathEconLib</h1>
    <p class="tagline">Machine-checked foundations for research and applications in economics.</p>
    <p class="lede">MathEconLib is an effort to build a shared formal foundation for economics in Lean 4. This is a public project co-founded by a team of economists and <a href="https://axiommath.ai">Axiom Math</a> in 2026.</p>
    <h2 id="research">Use existing theorems to prove new ones</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib provides shared definitions and reusable results that scholars and practitioners can apply and extend. Researchers can use these building blocks to refine existing economic frameworks, assemble new ones, and prove results about preferences, incentives, equilibrium, dynamics, identification, estimation, and inference.</p>
    <p>Library definitions and theorems support many economic models. Lean tracks the assumptions each proof relies on.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib builds on <a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4">Mathlib</a> and encourages upstream contributions of broadly reusable mathematical results.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib is not an end-to-end autoformalization project. Its definitions and conventions are developed by economic theorists, with the aim of building a trusted foundation for formal research in economics.</p>
    <p>We welcome researchers to use AI to help with formalization and proof development, but researchers determine the mathematical architecture and Lean's kernel checks every theorem. Contributions receive human review for consistency with our <a href="{{ site.repository }}/blob/{{ site.sourceBranch }}/DESIGN.md">design principles</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="vision">Long-term scope</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib is intended to cover all mathematical areas of economics, including:</p>
    <dl class="scope">
      <dt>Microeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Preferences and choice, games, information, contracts, market and mechanism design, industrial organization, and general equilibrium.</dd>
      <dt>Macroeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Dynamic optimization, recursive methods, growth, heterogeneous-agent models, and monetary and fiscal theory.</dd>
      <dt>Econometrics</dt>
      <dd>Probability and statistical foundations, identification, estimation, inference, and causal and structural methods.</dd>
    </dl>

